        summary: `Identify a text's central idea, main purpose, or key supporting details — and don't mistake a true-but-minor detail for the main point.`,
        concepts: [
          `Before looking at the choices, summarize the passage's main point in your own words in one sentence.`,
          `The central idea is what the passage is mostly about — not every fact it mentions along the way.`,
          `Wrong answers often lift a real detail from the text but present it as the main point, or overstate/understate what the passage actually says.`,
          `For "which detail best supports" questions, go back to the specific sentence(s) the question is pointing at rather than relying on memory.`,
          `Passages with a "turn" (however, but, in fact) often place the real central claim right after the pivot word — read past it before deciding on the main idea.`,
          `For paired "central idea + supporting detail" question sets, answer the main-idea question first; it often narrows which details even matter.`,
          `Skimming the topic sentence of each paragraph before reading the choices gives a fast, reliable check on your one-sentence summary.`,
        ],
